/* Original teaching notes and examples */
const COURSE = [
{
 id:'basics',title:'Your first Python program',stage:'Foundations',symbol:'01',image:'flow',summary:'Give a computer clear instructions. Start with a café bill and a few lines of Python.',analogy:'Think of a program as a recipe. Variables hold ingredients, operators combine them, and control flow decides which steps happen next. Python follows your instructions in order.',
 concepts:[
 ['Install Python & choose an editor','Install a supported Python 3 release from python.org. On Windows, open a fresh terminal and check py --version; on macOS or Linux, try python3 --version. IDLE is a simple editor commonly bundled with Python. VS Code is a general code editor; PyCharm is a Python IDE; Jupyter notebooks combine notes, code, and output. An IDE is a workspace for writing and debugging code.'],
 ['First program & basic syntax','Save print("Hello, Python!") in hello.py. Run py hello.py on Windows or python3 hello.py on macOS/Linux. Quotation marks create text; parentheses pass values to a function. A # starts a comment. Indentation groups statements: use four spaces consistently. Python is case-sensitive.'],
 ['Variables & native data types','A variable is a name referring to a value: price = 80. Python determines its type at runtime; no C-style declaration is required. int holds whole numbers, float decimal numbers, str text, bool True/False, and None means no value. Assignment (=) differs from equality comparison (==).'],
 ['Operators & input/output','Use +, -, *, / for arithmetic; // for floor division; % for remainders; ** for powers. Compare using ==, !=, <, <=, >, >=. Combine conditions using and, or, not. input() returns a string, so convert numeric input with int() or float(). print() displays values.'],
 ['Branching: if, elif, else & nested decisions','A condition selects a path. if checks the first condition, elif tries another, and else is the fallback. Add a colon and indent the body. A nested if belongs inside another branch, such as checking stock only after confirming a product exists.'],
 ['For, while & nested loops','A for loop visits items in a collection; range(3) produces 0, 1, 2. A while loop repeats while a condition is true, so update the condition to avoid an accidental endless loop. Nested loops visit combinations, like each row and column in a seating plan.'],
 ['break, continue & pass','break exits the nearest loop. continue skips the rest of the current iteration. pass does nothing and acts as a placeholder in an otherwise empty block. These three keywords have different jobs.'],
 ['Modules & imports','A module is a Python file whose definitions can be reused. import math makes math.sqrt(16) available. Keep related functions together in a file; avoid naming a file math.py or another standard library name because it may shadow the real module.']
 ],
 examples:[
 {title:'A café receipt',code:'price = 80\ncups = 3\ntotal = price * cups\n\nif total >= 200:\n    total = total - 20\n\nprint(f"Pay ₹{total}")',output:'Pay ₹220',explain:'price and cups hold numbers. Multiplication gives 240. The condition is true, so the café takes ₹20 off. An f-string inserts total into the message.'},
 {title:'Pack available orders',code:'orders = ["tea", "sold out", "coffee"]\nfor order in orders:\n    if order == "sold out":\n        continue\n    print("Packing", order)',output:'Packing tea\nPacking coffee',explain:'The loop visits every order. continue skips the unavailable item and moves to the next order.'}
 ],lab:{title:'Build your own café calculator',steps:['Ask for a price and quantity using input(). Convert them to float and int.','Apply a ₹20 discount when the bill is at least ₹200. Print the final amount.','Use a loop to print receipts for three customers. Try break, continue, and pass in separate experiments.'],hint:'Start with price = float(input("Price: ")). Test bills below, equal to, and above 200. Reject negative prices and quantities; catch conversion errors after module 08.'},quiz:{q:'What does input() return, before you convert it?',options:['A string (text)','An integer','Always a floating-point number'],answer:0,why:'input() returns text. Use int() or float() when you need a number.'},source:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/introduction.html'
},
{
 id:'strings',title:'Strings & everyday text',stage:'Foundations',symbol:'Aa',image:'collection',summary:'Clean names, format messages, and slice text into useful pieces.',analogy:'A string is like a row of letter tiles. Each tile has a position, starting at zero. You can take a slice of the row, but you create new text rather than changing the original tiles.',
 concepts:[['Accessing strings & indexing','name[0] gets the first character; name[-1] gets the last. An out-of-range index raises IndexError. len(name) counts characters. Strings are immutable: name[0] = "A" is not allowed.'],['Basic operations','+ joins strings and * repeats them. "tea" in menu checks whether text occurs. == compares values. Methods such as strip(), lower(), upper(), and replace() return new strings; assign their result if you want to keep it.'],['String slices','text[start:stop:step] selects a slice. The stop is excluded. "Python"[0:3] is "Pyt"; text[::-1] reverses the string. Missing start/stop values use the beginning/end.'],['Assign multiple values at once','first, last = "Asha", "Rao" assigns two values in one statement. Unpacking also works with a tuple: name, age = ("Asha", 21). The number of variables must match the number of values unless you use starred unpacking.'],['Formatting strings','f"Hello {name}" inserts a value. f"{price:.2f}" shows two decimal places. str.format() and %-formatting are older styles you will see in existing code. Choose a consistent style; f-strings are convenient for new examples.'],['Historical string methods & tuples','Older Python examples sometimes use the string module for operations now available as str methods, such as text.lower(). Python 3 text is Unicode. Tuples can group related values, such as (first_name, last_name); the dedicated tuple module explains their behavior.']],
 examples:[{title:'Clean a customer name',code:'raw_name = "  asha rao  "\nname = raw_name.strip().title()\nprint(f"Welcome, {name}!")\nprint(name[:4])',output:'Welcome, Asha Rao!\nAsha',explain:'strip removes surrounding whitespace. title capitalizes words. [:4] takes positions 0 through 3.'},{title:'Format a price label',code:'item, price = "Notebook", 49.5\nprint(f"{item}: ₹{price:.2f}")',output:'Notebook: ₹49.50',explain:'Two variables receive two values. :.2f gives the price two decimal places.'}],lab:{title:'Make tidy contact labels',steps:['Store a name with extra spaces and inconsistent capitalization.','Clean it, then print a greeting and the first three characters.','Format a price with two decimal places and split a full name into words.'],hint:'Use strip(), title(), [:3], and split(). Always keep a separate raw value if you need the original input.'},quiz:{q:'What is "Python"[1:4]?',options:['Pyt','yth','ytho'],answer:1,why:'Index 1 is y. The slice includes indices 1, 2, and 3, stopping before index 4.'},source:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/introduction.html#strings'
},
{
 id:'dictionaries',title:'Dictionaries: label your data',stage:'Foundations',symbol:'{}',image:'collection',summary:'Use meaningful keys to organize customer profiles, products, and settings.',analogy:'A dictionary is a labeled filing cabinet. Instead of remembering a drawer number, you ask for a label such as "name" or "email". Each unique key points to one value.',
 concepts:[['Define a dictionary','Use braces and key: value pairs: product = {"name": "Tea", "price": 80}. Keys must be hashable, such as strings or numbers. Values can be any Python objects. Dictionaries preserve insertion order.'],['Access & introduce keys','product["price"] retrieves a value but raises KeyError if the key is absent. product.get("stock", 0) supplies a fallback. "price" in product checks keys, not values.'],['Modify a dictionary','product["price"] = 90 changes an existing value. product["stock"] = 12 adds a new key. update() combines multiple changes. Reusing a key replaces its old value.'],['Delete items','del product["stock"] removes a key and raises KeyError if it is missing. product.pop("stock", None) removes it with a fallback. clear() empties the dictionary.'],['Loop through records','for key, value in product.items(): visits key/value pairs. keys() and values() provide views of keys and values. Do not change dictionary size while iterating over it.']],
 examples:[{title:'Update a shop product',code:'product = {"name": "Tea", "price": 80}\nproduct["stock"] = 12\nproduct["price"] = 90\nprint(product["name"], product["price"])\nprint(product.get("discount", 0))',output:'Tea 90\n0',explain:'Named keys make the record easy to read. get returns 0 when there is no discount key.'},{title:'Remove an outdated setting',code:'settings = {"theme": "light", "old_mode": True}\nsettings.pop("old_mode", None)\nfor key, value in settings.items():\n    print(key, value)',output:'theme light',explain:'pop removes a key. items supplies the remaining key/value pairs.'}],lab:{title:'Create a contact card',steps:['Define a contact dictionary with name, phone, and city.','Change the city, add an email, and retrieve an absent key using get().','Delete the phone field and print each remaining key/value pair.'],hint:'Use contact["city"] = "Pune" and contact.get("nickname", "No nickname"). Avoid storing actual private contact information in practice files.'},quiz:{q:'What happens when you assign a new value to an existing dictionary key?',options:['It creates a second identical key','It replaces the previous value','It always raises an error'],answer:1,why:'Each key is unique within the dictionary. Assignment updates the value for that key.'},source:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries'
},
{
 id:'lists',title:'Lists: organize & transform',stage:'Foundations',symbol:'[]',image:'collection',summary:'Build a shopping basket, search items, and transform a collection of prices.',analogy:'A list is a shopping basket with numbered positions. You can add items, remove items, reorder them, and walk through them one by one. Unlike a string, a list can be changed in place.',
 concepts:[['Define, assign & retrieve','items = ["milk", "bread"] defines a list. items[0] retrieves the first value. items[1] = "rice" replaces the second. Lists can mix types, although consistent item types often make analysis easier.'],['Indexing & operations','Use len(items), items[start:stop], + to concatenate, and * to repeat. Negative indices count from the end. Copies made by slicing are shallow: nested objects are still shared.'],['Add elements','append(x) adds one object at the end. extend(iterable) adds each supplied item. insert(index, x) places an item at a position. append([1, 2]) adds a nested list; extend([1, 2]) adds two numbers.'],['Search & delete','x in items checks membership. index(x) finds the first match and raises ValueError if absent. remove(x) deletes the first matching value. pop(index) removes and returns an item; del items[index] removes by position.'],['List operators & mapping','A comprehension such as [price * 2 for price in prices] transforms each element. Add if to filter elements. map(function, items) is another transformation tool and returns an iterator; list(...) collects its results.'],['Join lists & split strings','Lists join with + or extend(). "a,b,c".split(",") produces a list of words. ", ".join(words) makes a string from a list of strings. Convert numbers with str() before joining them.'],['Sort safely','sorted(items) returns a sorted new list. items.sort() changes the original and returns None. Avoid assigning items = items.sort(), which would replace your list with None.']],
 examples:[{title:'Manage a shopping basket',code:'basket = ["milk", "bread"]\nbasket.append("apples")\nbasket.remove("bread")\nprint(", ".join(basket))',output:'milk, apples',explain:'The list changes when you append or remove items. join combines its text values into one display string.'},{title:'Apply a discount to prices',code:'prices = [100, 200, 50]\ndiscounted = [price * 0.9 for price in prices]\nprint(discounted)\nprint(sum(discounted))',output:'[90.0, 180.0, 45.0]\n315.0',explain:'The comprehension maps each price to 90% of its original value. sum adds the results.'}],lab:{title:'Build an expense tracker',steps:['Store five sample daily expenses in a list.','Add a new expense, remove one by position, and find the total.','Create a list containing only expenses above 100, then sort a copy.'],hint:'Try [x for x in expenses if x > 100]. Compare sorted(expenses) with expenses.sort().'},quiz:{q:'Which method adds each item from another list?',options:['append()','extend()','get()'],answer:1,why:'extend adds individual elements. append adds its entire argument as a single element.'},source:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html'
},
{
 id:'functions',title:'Functions, methods & patterns',stage:'Foundations',symbol:'ƒ',image:'flow',summary:'Turn repeated steps into reusable recipes, then find patterns in text.',analogy:'A function is a reusable recipe: ingredients go in as arguments and a result comes out through return. A method is a function attached to an object, such as a string’s strip() method.',
 concepts:[['Define & call functions','def names a function. Parameters name the expected inputs; arguments are the actual values passed at a call. return sends a value back and ends that call. A function without an explicit return returns None.'],['Function types & arguments','You can use built-in functions such as len(), user-defined functions with def, and anonymous lambda expressions. Positional arguments match by order; keyword arguments match by parameter name. *args gathers extra positional arguments; **kwargs gathers extra named ones.'],['Optional & named arguments','def greet(name, prefix="Hello"): makes prefix optional. greet("Asha", prefix="Hi") uses a named argument. Avoid mutable defaults such as items=[]; use None and create a new list inside instead.'],['Local & global variables','A name assigned inside a function is usually local to that function. Names at module level are global. Reading a global is possible; rebinding it inside a function needs global. Prefer explicit parameters and return values to surprising global changes.'],['Anonymous functions','lambda x: x * 2 defines a small single-expression function. It is useful as a key for sorting. Use def when the logic needs multiple steps or a helpful descriptive name.'],['type, str, dir & other built-ins','type(value) shows its type, str(value) converts it to text, dir(value) lists available names, and help(value) explains usage. len(), sum(), min(), max(), and sorted() are useful daily tools.'],['Regular expressions','import re provides pattern matching. re.search finds a match anywhere; re.fullmatch checks the whole string. Use raw strings for patterns: r"[0-9]+" matches one or more digits. Match objects have group(); no match returns None. Simple patterns are not complete validation rules for all real-world formats.']],
 examples:[{title:'Reuse a delivery calculation',code:'def delivery_total(price, fee=30):\n    return price + fee\n\nprint(delivery_total(200))\nprint(delivery_total(200, fee=0))',output:'230\n200',explain:'The optional fee defaults to 30. The second call names fee and overrides it with 0.'},{title:'Read an order number',code:'import re\nmessage = "Order #204 is ready"\nmatch = re.search(r"#([0-9]+)", message)\nif match:\n    print(match.group(1))',output:'204',explain:'The parentheses capture the digits after #. The if check avoids using group() when there is no match.'}],lab:{title:'Write reusable receipt helpers',steps:['Define a function that takes a price and an optional discount percentage.','Call it once with positional arguments and once with named arguments.','Extract an order number from text with re.search; also test text without a number.'],hint:'Return price * (1 - discount / 100). Keep the calculation separate from print().'},quiz:{q:'What sends a calculated value back to a function’s caller?',options:['print','return','pass'],answer:1,why:'return supplies the result to the caller. print only displays text.'},source:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#defining-functions'
},
{
 id:'tuples',title:'Tuples: keep values together',stage:'Foundations',symbol:'()',image:'collection',summary:'Represent coordinates, fixed settings, and multiple function results.',analogy:'A tuple is a sealed tray holding several values in order. You can read each compartment, but you cannot replace, add, or remove compartments after the tuple is created.',
 concepts:[['Introduce & define tuples','point = (10, 20) creates a tuple. The comma makes the tuple: single = (10,) is a one-item tuple, while (10) is just the number 10. Empty tuples use ().'],['Access tuples','point[0] gets the first item. Negative indices and slices work as they do with lists. x, y = point unpacks a pair into meaningful names.'],['Operations & immutability','len(), in, +, and * work on tuples. You cannot assign point[0] = 5. A tuple containing a list can still hold a list that changes: immutability applies to the tuple’s references, not recursively to every contained object.'],['Returning multiple values','return latitude, longitude returns a tuple that the caller can unpack. Use tuples for a fixed group of related values and lists when items need frequent changes.']],
 examples:[{title:'Store a delivery location',code:'location = (18.52, 73.86)\nlatitude, longitude = location\nprint(f"Latitude: {latitude}")\nprint(f"Longitude: {longitude}")',output:'Latitude: 18.52\nLongitude: 73.86',explain:'Two related coordinates travel as a pair. Unpacking gives each a clear name.'},{title:'Return the smallest and largest expense',code:'def limits(values):\n    return min(values), max(values)\n\nlow, high = limits([80, 240, 120])\nprint(low, high)',output:'80 240',explain:'A single return groups two results into a tuple, then the caller unpacks them.'}],lab:{title:'Create a location directory',steps:['Define three locations as latitude/longitude tuples.','Put the tuples in a list and print each pair using unpacking.','Experiment with (5) and (5,) and inspect their types.'],hint:'print(type((5))) shows int; print(type((5,))) shows tuple.'},quiz:{q:'Which expression creates a one-item tuple?',options:['(5)','[5]','(5,)'],answer:2,why:'The trailing comma distinguishes a one-item tuple from a parenthesized number.'},source:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#tuples-and-sequences'
},
{
 id:'objects',title:'Objects & reusable blueprints',stage:'Build & debug',symbol:'◇',image:'object',summary:'Model real things with classes, attributes, inheritance, and decorators.',analogy:'A class is a blueprint for a bicycle. Each object is a particular bicycle built from that blueprint. Objects have their own attributes, while methods describe actions they can perform.',
 concepts:[['What is an object?','Python values are objects: numbers, strings, lists, and functions all have a type and behavior. A class defines a new type. An instance is an object created from that class. OOP organizes state and behavior together.'],['Variables, references & native types','Assignment binds a name to an object rather than copying it. If a = [1] and b = a, both names refer to the same list; b.append(2) also changes what a sees. a.copy() makes a shallow copy. is checks identity; == checks equality.'],['Class, object & attributes','class names a blueprint. __init__ initializes a new instance; self refers to that instance in a method. self.name is an instance attribute. Class attributes are shared through the class; instance attributes belong to each object.'],['Inheritance & overriding','A subclass reuses and extends a parent class. class ElectricBike(Bike): inherits from Bike. A subclass method with the same name overrides the parent behavior. super() accesses parent methods, often for initialization.'],['Overloading in Python','Defining the same method name twice does not create multiple signatures; the later definition replaces the earlier one. Default arguments or dispatch can support multiple calling patterns. Operator overloading uses special methods such as __add__ for +.'],['Decorators','A decorator wraps or transforms a function or class. @decorator above a definition applies it. functools.wraps preserves metadata when wrapping a function. @property exposes a method through attribute access, useful for computed values.'],['Data hiding & encapsulation','A leading underscore signals an internal implementation detail. Double-leading underscores trigger name mangling, which reduces accidental name collisions rather than providing a security boundary. Properties can validate changes to state. Python does not enforce truly private attributes.']],
 examples:[{title:'A product blueprint',code:'class Product:\n    def __init__(self, name, price):\n        self.name = name\n        self.price = price\n\n    def label(self):\n        return f"{self.name}: ₹{self.price}"\n\ntea = Product("Tea", 80)\nprint(tea.label())',output:'Tea: ₹80',explain:'Product defines the structure. tea is one instance with its own name and price; label is a method.'},{title:'A computed attribute',code:'class Basket:\n    def __init__(self, prices):\n        self.prices = prices\n\n    @property\n    def total(self):\n        return sum(self.prices)\n\nbasket = Basket([80, 120])\nprint(basket.total)',output:'200',explain:'@property lets total be read like an attribute while calculating it from the current list.'}],lab:{title:'Model a library book',steps:['Create a Book class with title and is_borrowed attributes.','Add borrow() and return_book() methods; reject a second borrow.','Create an EBook subclass that overrides a description method. Add a @property for availability.'],hint:'Use self.is_borrowed to hold the current state. Call super().__init__(title) in a subclass if it needs the parent initialization.'},quiz:{q:'After a = [1]; b = a; b.append(2), what does a contain?',options:['[1]','[1, 2]','[2]'],answer:1,why:'a and b refer to the same mutable list. Assignment did not create a copy.'},source:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/classes.html'
},
{
 id:'exceptions',title:'Handle errors with confidence',stage:'Build & debug',symbol:'!',image:'flow',summary:'Keep a program useful when input is invalid or something goes wrong.',analogy:'Exception handling is a backup plan. If a recipe fails because an ingredient is missing, you handle that particular problem. A finally block is the cleanup step that happens whether the recipe succeeds or fails.',
 concepts:[['try & except clauses','Place a risky operation inside try and handle a specific error in except. float("hello") raises ValueError. Catch the errors you can meaningfully handle; broad catch-all handlers may hide programming mistakes.'],['Multiple exceptions & else','Use separate except clauses for different recovery steps, or except (ValueError, TypeError) for a shared handler. else runs only when the try body finishes without raising an exception. Keep the try block small.'],['try/finally & cleanup','finally runs when leaving the try statement under normal execution, including when an exception propagates. Use it to release resources. A with statement is often simpler for files and other context-managed resources.'],['Raise & user-defined exceptions','raise ValueError("message") signals invalid input. Define a custom error by subclassing Exception, such as class OutOfStockError(Exception): pass. Give the error a useful message and let the caller decide how to recover.']],
 examples:[{title:'Handle a mistyped quantity',code:'raw_quantity = "two"\ntry:\n    quantity = int(raw_quantity)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print("Please enter a whole number.")\nfinally:\n    print("Input check finished.")',output:'Please enter a whole number.\nInput check finished.',explain:'int cannot convert this word. except handles the conversion failure, and finally runs afterward.'},{title:'Explain an unavailable product',code:'class OutOfStockError(Exception):\n    pass\n\ntry:\n    stock = 0\n    if stock == 0:\n        raise OutOfStockError("Tea is unavailable")\nexcept OutOfStockError as error:\n    print(error)',output:'Tea is unavailable',explain:'The custom exception names the business problem clearly and carries a readable message.'}],lab:{title:'Make the calculator resilient',steps:['Accept a quantity and catch invalid integer input.','Reject negative quantities with a useful exception message.','Try an empty string, a word, zero, and a valid number.'],hint:'Catch ValueError rather than every exception. An invalid input is not the same as a valid zero.'},quiz:{q:'Which block performs cleanup whether try succeeds or raises?',options:['else','finally','pass'],answer:1,why:'finally handles cleanup when leaving the try statement. else runs only after a successful try.'},source:'https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/errors.html'
},
{
 id:'libraries',title:'Libraries, debugging & logging',stage:'Build & debug',symbol:'⚙',image:'flow',summary:'Use existing tools, trace mistakes, and leave useful clues for your future self.',analogy:'Libraries are a shared toolbox. Debugging is following a trail to find the broken step. Logging is keeping a dated workshop notebook of what your program did.',
 concepts:[['Standard & third-party libraries','The standard library ships with Python, including math, pathlib, logging, and sqlite3. Third-party packages such as pandas are installed separately. Use a project virtual environment to isolate dependencies: py -m venv .venv on Windows, or python3 -m venv .venv elsewhere.'],['Install packages in your environment','After creating .venv, install with .venv\\Scripts\\python.exe -m pip install numpy pandas on Windows, or .venv/bin/python -m pip install numpy pandas on macOS/Linux. This explicitly uses your project interpreter and avoids mixing installations.'],['Functional programming','Functions can be passed as values. map transforms items, filter keeps matching items, and functools.reduce combines them. Comprehensions are often easier to read. A pure function depends on its inputs and avoids changing external state.'],['Debugging basics','Reproduce the problem with a small input. Read the traceback from the final error line upward. Inspect variable values, use editor breakpoints, or run python -m pdb script.py with your interpreter. Distinguish syntax errors, runtime exceptions, and incorrect results.'],['Logging with Python','Use logging.getLogger(__name__) and logger.info(...) instead of scattered print statements in larger programs. Levels include DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, and CRITICAL. basicConfig configures a simple logger. Do not record passwords or sensitive inputs.']],
 examples:[{title:'A traceable calculation',code:'import logging\nlogging.basicConfig(\n    level=logging.INFO,\n    format="%(levelname)s: %(message)s"\n)\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\nprices = [80, 120]\nlogger.info("Processed %s items", len(prices))\nprint(sum(prices))',output:'INFO: Processed 2 items\n200',explain:'The log states what happened; print displays the result. Logging normally writes to stderr, so display order between log and print may vary.'},{title:'Transform without changing the original',code:'def add_delivery(price):\n    return price + 30\n\nprices = [80, 120]\ntotals = list(map(add_delivery, prices))\nprint(totals)\nprint(prices)',output:'[110, 150]\n[80, 120]',explain:'map calls add_delivery for each price. The function returns a new number rather than changing the input list.'}],lab:{title:'Find and explain a bug',steps:['Create a function with an incorrect discount calculation.','Use three known inputs and inspect intermediate values to find the mistake.','Fix it and add INFO logging for completed calculations and WARNING for invalid input.'],hint:'For a 10% discount on 100, expect 90. Check whether you accidentally subtract 10% as 0.1 instead of multiplying by 0.9.'},quiz:{q:'Which logging level normally describes an expected completed operation?',options:['INFO','CRITICAL','ERROR'],answer:0,why:'INFO records normal events. ERROR and CRITICAL describe failures.'},source:'https://docs.python.org/3/howto/logging.html'
},
{
 id:'data',title:'NumPy, SciPy & Pandas',stage:'Data & AI',symbol:'▦',image:'data',summary:'Turn messy rows into useful numbers, summaries, and features for AI.',analogy:'A Python list is a general basket. A NumPy array is a numerical tray built for operations across many cells. A Pandas DataFrame is a labeled spreadsheet whose columns you can clean, select, and group.',
 concepts:[['NumPy arrays vs Python lists','A typical NumPy array stores values with a consistent dtype and supports element-wise numeric operations. [1, 2] * 2 repeats a list, but np.array([1, 2]) * 2 multiplies each value. Vectorization can reduce Python-level loop overhead, though performance depends on workload and size.'],['Shape, indexing & transformations','array.shape describes dimensions. Slice rows and columns to select values. Use astype to convert types and arithmetic for feature transformations. Normalize using meaningful scales; when training a model, learn preprocessing parameters on training data to avoid leaking test information.'],['SciPy tools','SciPy builds on NumPy and provides scientific algorithms for optimization, statistics, integration, and signal processing. For example, scipy.stats describes probability distributions and scipy.optimize finds solutions to numerical problems.'],['Pandas Series & DataFrames','A Series is a labeled one-dimensional collection; a DataFrame is a table of columns. pd.DataFrame({...}) constructs a table. df["sales"] selects a column; df.loc[...] selects by labels and df.iloc[...] by position.'],['Load data & meaningful names','pd.read_csv("sales.csv") reads a local CSV. pd.read_csv(url) can read a directly downloadable CSV from a URL. Inspect head(), shape, info(), and dtypes. Use rename(columns={...}) or names= when a file lacks a header; confirm column order and units.'],['Wrangle missing values & duplicates','isna() identifies missing values. dropna() removes them; fillna() replaces them using an explicitly chosen rule. drop_duplicates() removes duplicate rows. Convert numeric strings with pd.to_numeric(errors="coerce") and inspect the values that became missing.'],['Group & aggregate','df.groupby("product")["sales"].sum() combines rows with matching products. agg() can compute multiple statistics. Check whether a sum, mean, or count answers your question and whether missing values affect interpretation.']],
 examples:[{title:'Turn raw text into numerical features',code:'import numpy as np\nraw = "10,20,30"\nvalues = np.array([float(x) for x in raw.split(",")])\nscaled = values / 10\nprint(scaled.tolist())',output:'[1.0, 2.0, 3.0]',explain:'split produces strings; float converts each to a number. NumPy divides every value by 10. Production code should validate empty and nonnumeric fields.'},{title:'Summarize shop sales',code:'import pandas as pd\nsales = pd.DataFrame({\n    "product": ["Tea", "Coffee", "Tea"],\n    "sales": [100, 150, 80]\n})\ntotals = sales.groupby("product")["sales"].sum()\nprint(totals.to_dict())',output:"{'Coffee': 150, 'Tea': 180}",explain:'Grouping collects the two Tea rows, then sum combines 100 and 80. These are illustrative sample sales.'}],lab:{title:'Clean a small sales dataset',steps:['Create a CSV with product, year, and sales, including one missing value and one duplicate.','Load it into Pandas, inspect types, rename unclear columns, and choose a documented cleanup rule.','Group sales by product and year. Explain why your chosen aggregation fits the question.'],hint:'Keep raw data separate. Compare the row counts before and after cleaning. Install numpy, scipy, and pandas in your virtual environment.'},self:['Compare a list comprehension with a NumPy transformation using time.perf_counter(); repeat runs and report array size and setup costs.','Convert raw user input to a numeric array with explicit validation. Simulate scaling a feature and compare execution time.','Load a directly downloadable real-world CSV URL, record its source and retrieval date, and assign readable column names.','Group and aggregate the data, then visualize and document a trend and a limitation.'],quiz:{q:'What does np.array([1, 2]) * 2 do?',options:['Creates [1, 2, 1, 2]','Creates an array containing 2 and 4','Sorts the values'],answer:1,why:'NumPy multiplication is element-wise. A Python list multiplied by 2 repeats its elements.'},source:'https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/getting_started/'
},
{
 id:'visualization',title:'Make data tell a clear story',stage:'Data & AI',symbol:'▥',image:'chart',summary:'Choose charts that answer a question, from yearly sales to product comparisons.',analogy:'A chart is a window into a table. A line reveals change over time, a bar compares categories, and a scatter plot shows how two measurements move together. Choose the window that answers your question.',
 concepts:[['Select an appropriate chart','Use bars for category comparisons, lines for ordered time trends, histograms for distributions, and scatter plots for relationships. Label units and axes. Bar charts usually need a zero baseline to preserve meaningful length comparisons. Correlation alone does not show causation.'],['Matplotlib: explicit control','Matplotlib creates static plots and supports detailed customization. fig, ax = plt.subplots() gives a figure and axes. Set labels and titles, then plt.show() to display or fig.savefig(...) to export.'],['Seaborn: statistical graphics','Seaborn works with tabular data and builds on Matplotlib. It simplifies distributions and grouped comparisons. Functions such as barplot may aggregate observations; understand the estimator and uncertainty display before interpreting the result.'],['ggplot-style graphics','The syllabus names ggplot. In Python, plotnine implements a grammar-of-graphics approach with data, aesthetics, and layers; Matplotlib also has a ggplot style. These are distinct tools. Layer a scatter plot and a trend line deliberately.'],['Plotly: interactive charts','Plotly supports hover details, zooming, and interactive HTML output. plotly.express is a convenient interface for tables. Interactive charts may have a larger delivery footprint than a static image; choose based on the audience and task.'],['Translate visuals into conclusions','State the pattern, quote a supporting number, and note a limitation. Compare the same units and time periods. A chart of revenue alone does not prove profit increased or explain why sales changed.']],
 examples:[{title:'Compare product sales',code:'import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nproducts = ["Tea", "Coffee", "Juice"]\nsales = [180, 150, 90]\nfig, ax = plt.subplots()\nax.bar(products, sales, color="#1b514b")\nax.set_ylabel("Sales (₹)")\nax.set_title("Illustrative shop sales")\nplt.show()',output:'A bar chart with Tea = ₹180, Coffee = ₹150, and Juice = ₹90.',explain:'Categories are compared by height. Tea exceeds Coffee by ₹30 in this small sample.'},{title:'Inspect a yearly trend interactively',code:'import pandas as pd\nimport plotly.express as px\ndata = pd.DataFrame({\n    "year": [2024, 2025, 2026],\n    "sales": [120, 150, 180]\n})\nfig = px.line(data, x="year", y="sales", markers=True)\nfig.show()',output:'An interactive line chart connects sales values 120 → 150 → 180.',explain:'Year has a natural order, so a line helps show change. Hovering in the generated chart reveals the values.'}],lab:{title:'Tell one sales story three ways',steps:['Create sample sales for two products over three years.','Build a Matplotlib bar chart, a Seaborn comparison, and a Plotly line chart.','Add labels and units. Write two observations supported by numbers and one limitation.'],hint:'Install matplotlib, seaborn, plotly, and plotnine as needed. Use a consistent scale and avoid claiming the illustrative data describes a real business.'},self:['Select visualization types based on the question and data, and document your reasoning.','Compare years and products as you would in a sales or KPI dashboard.','Compare static Matplotlib, statistical Seaborn, and interactive Plotly results for readability and delivery size.','Translate the visuals into simple analytical conclusions, with a supporting number and a limitation.'],quiz:{q:'Which chart is usually a good starting point for sales over consecutive years?',options:['A line chart','A pie chart for each individual transaction','A chart without axis labels'],answer:0,why:'A line emphasizes an ordered trend over time. Label the axes and units to make it understandable.'},source:'https://matplotlib.org/stable/tutorials/index.html'
},
{
 id:'databases',title:'Databases & reliable records',stage:'Data & AI',symbol:'DB',image:'data',summary:'Save records, use SQL, and prevent common data integrity problems.',analogy:'A database is an organized ledger that survives when a program closes. Tables hold rows, constraints enforce rules, and transactions keep a related group of changes consistent.',
 concepts:[['Databases in Python','SQLite is a file-based SQL database available through the standard sqlite3 module. Larger applications may use PostgreSQL or another database. A connection talks to the database; execute runs SQL statements. An in-memory database is temporary.'],['CRUD operations','Create inserts a row with INSERT. Read retrieves rows with SELECT. Update changes rows with UPDATE. Delete removes rows with DELETE. Use a WHERE condition for targeted updates and deletions.'],['Parameterized SQL','Pass values separately: connection.execute("SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ?", (email,)). Placeholders keep data separate from SQL. Never build SQL by concatenating raw user input. A one-value parameter tuple needs a trailing comma.'],['Transactions & cleanup','Commit to persist a transaction or rollback to discard it. A sqlite3 connection used with with commits on success and rolls back on failure, but that context does not close the connection. Close it explicitly or use contextlib.closing.'],['Integrity, missing values & duplicates','PRIMARY KEY identifies a row. UNIQUE prevents duplicate values, and NOT NULL rejects SQL NULL. Validate blank strings separately. Database constraints remain necessary even when application code checks for an existing record.'],['AI application backends','Databases can store users, model job metadata, and prediction records. Keep schema names meaningful, avoid unnecessary sensitive data, and document how records are related. Check-then-insert alone can race under concurrent use.']],
 examples:[{title:'Store and retrieve a sample user',code:'import sqlite3\nfrom contextlib import closing\n\nwith closing(sqlite3.connect(":memory:")) as db:\n    with db:\n        db.execute("CREATE TABLE users (email TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL)")\n        db.execute("INSERT INTO users VALUES (?)", ("asha@example.com",))\n    row = db.execute("SELECT email FROM users").fetchone()\n    print(row[0])',output:'asha@example.com',explain:':memory: keeps this demo temporary. The placeholder separates data from SQL; the constraints prevent duplicate or NULL emails.'},{title:'Update a product safely',code:'import sqlite3\nfrom contextlib import closing\n\nwith closing(sqlite3.connect(":memory:")) as db:\n    with db:\n        db.execute("CREATE TABLE products (name TEXT, price REAL)")\n        db.execute("INSERT INTO products VALUES (?, ?)", ("Tea", 80))\n        db.execute("UPDATE products SET price = ? WHERE name = ?", (90, "Tea"))\n    print(db.execute("SELECT price FROM products").fetchone()[0])',output:'90.0',explain:'WHERE limits the update to Tea. Values are passed separately rather than interpolated into SQL.'}],lab:{title:'Build a small user directory',steps:['Use a local SQLite file. Create a users table with a primary key and unique email.','Perform insert, select, update, and delete using placeholders.','Validate blank input, handle sqlite3.IntegrityError on duplicates, and confirm changes persist after reopening.'],hint:'Use example.com addresses. Keep a UNIQUE constraint even if you first SELECT to check whether a record exists.'},self:['Explain how databases support user management and AI backends.','Perform CRUD operations with Python and SQL.','Validate an existing record and insert new sample user data safely, including constraint error handling.','Identify duplicates and missing values and document the integrity rule that prevents each problem.'],quiz:{q:'Which is the safe pattern for inserting user-supplied values into SQL?',options:['Concatenate input into the SQL string','Use SQL placeholders and pass parameters separately','Remove spaces from the input only'],answer:1,why:'Parameterized queries separate SQL syntax from data. Basic string cleanup is not a substitute.'},source:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/sqlite3.html'
},
{
 id:'web',title:'Flask, Django & web services',stage:'Data & AI',symbol:'↗',image:'web',summary:'Understand how a browser calls Python and how an AI model becomes a service.',analogy:'A web service is like a restaurant counter. A request brings an order, a route sends it to the right function, and a response returns the result. Your model can live in the kitchen behind that counter.',
 concepts:[['HTTP requests & routes','A browser or client sends an HTTP request to a URL. A route maps a path and method to code. GET commonly retrieves information; POST submits data. Responses include a status code and a body such as JSON or HTML.'],['Flask: a lightweight framework','Flask offers a small core for routes and responses and lets you choose additional components. It suits small services and APIs. Install Flask in your environment and start a development app with python -m flask --app app run, using the appropriate interpreter.'],['Django: a full-stack framework','Django includes URL routing, templates, an ORM for database models, forms, authentication tools, and an admin interface. A project contains configuration; apps group features. It suits larger web applications with integrated components.'],['Expose an AI model','Load a trained model once where appropriate. Validate request schema, convert values to expected features, call the model, and return JSON. Keep preprocessing consistent with training. A working web endpoint is not itself a trained AI model.'],['Validation & production behavior','Validate types, ranges, and missing fields. Return useful 4xx status codes for invalid requests and handle unexpected failures. Development servers are for development; production needs an appropriate deployment server, configuration, and operational controls.']],
 examples:[{title:'A simple Flask JSON endpoint',code:'from flask import Flask\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.get("/hello")\ndef hello():\n    return {"message": "Hello, Python!"}',output:'GET /hello responds with JSON: {"message":"Hello, Python!"}',explain:'Save as app.py, install flask, and run it with the Flask CLI. @app.get connects a URL to a function.'},{title:'A Django view',code:'# views.py\nfrom django.http import JsonResponse\n\ndef hello(request):\n    return JsonResponse({"message": "Hello, Python!"})\n\n# In your Django app\'s urls.py:\nfrom django.urls import path\nurlpatterns = [path("hello/", hello)]',output:'After wiring this app into the project URLs, GET /hello/ returns the greeting JSON.',explain:'This is a view and URL snippet for an existing Django project, not a standalone script. Add the app URL configuration with include() in the project urls.py.'}],lab:{title:'Build a delivery estimate endpoint',steps:['Create a Flask app with a POST /estimate route that accepts JSON distance.','Validate that distance is a nonnegative number, then return a clearly labeled rule-based estimate.','Test missing fields and invalid types. Sketch how a trained model would replace the rule.'],hint:'Use request.get_json(), validate before calculating, and return (error_dict, 400) for bad input. Exclude bool when expecting a numeric value.'},self:['Diagram request → validation → preprocessing → model → JSON response.','Compare Flask’s small core with Django’s integrated full-stack tools.','Explain what you would need to expose an actual trained AI model through a web service.'],quiz:{q:'What does a web route connect?',options:['A URL and HTTP method to handler code','A Python list to a tuple','Only a database table to a chart'],answer:0,why:'Routing determines which handler processes a request to a particular path and method.'},source:'https://flask.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/quickstart/'
},
{
 id:'web-data',title:'Requests, urllib & web data',stage:'Data & AI',symbol:'www',image:'web',summary:'Fetch external data and understand responsible collection with Scrapy.',analogy:'An API is a clearly labeled service window for data. Scraping is reading information from a web page’s structure. Both require permission, sensible request rates, and care about how the data will be used.',
 concepts:[['Requests library','Requests is a third-party HTTP client. requests.get(url, timeout=10) fetches data; raise_for_status() checks HTTP errors; json() parses a JSON body. Set a timeout, handle connection errors, and validate the returned structure.'],['urllib in the standard library','urllib.request.urlopen fetches URLs without installing Requests. Use a with block to close the response. urllib.parse.urlencode creates query strings safely. Decode response bytes with the correct encoding before treating them as text.'],['Scrapy & spiders','The supplied syllabus spells the tool "scrappy"; the established Python scraping framework is Scrapy. A spider defines where to start and how to extract items. CSS or XPath selectors find structured content. A Scrapy project also includes settings and item pipelines.'],['APIs vs scraping','Prefer a documented API or a permitted downloadable dataset when available. HTML structure can change and break selectors; some pages render data through JavaScript. Record the source, collection date, units, and extraction assumptions.'],['Ethical considerations & limitations','Check a site’s terms, access permissions, and robots.txt guidance; robots rules alone are not legal permission. Respect rate limits and privacy. Do not bypass sign-in or collect unnecessary personal data. Consider licensing, completeness, and sampling bias before using collected data.'],['Dataset creation & market analysis','Web data can inform a dataset or a product comparison, but pages may be missing, stale, or biased. Validate values, deduplicate records, and label a data collection exercise as illustrative until its quality has been established.']],
 examples:[{title:'Fetch a public sample API',code:'import requests\n\nurl = "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1"\nresponse = requests.get(url, timeout=10)\nresponse.raise_for_status()\ntodo = response.json()\nprint(todo["id"])',output:'Expected sample API value: 1. This requires internet access and the service to be available.',explain:'The timeout limits waiting and raise_for_status flags HTTP errors. The example API contains placeholder data, not real user records.'},{title:'Extract a title from local sample HTML',code:'from scrapy import Selector\n\nhtml = "<article><h2>Tea</h2><span>80</span></article>"\npage = Selector(text=html)\nprint(page.css("h2::text").get())',output:'Tea',explain:'A CSS selector extracts text from a tiny local HTML sample. No external site is scraped in this example.'}],lab:{title:'Collect and document permitted sample data',steps:['Install requests and scrapy in your environment. Fetch a public sample API with a timeout and handle requests.RequestException.','Use a local HTML file with three products and extract names and prices using Scrapy selectors.','Save the results as CSV and document source, assumptions, missing values, and duplicates.'],hint:'Start with local HTML so you can practice extraction without repeatedly requesting another website. For an actual spider, configure reasonable delays and robots rules.'},self:['Describe how data is collected from external web sources and when to prefer an API.','Document ethical considerations, site limitations, permissions, and privacy concerns.','Explain how collected data could support dataset creation or market analysis, and identify possible bias.'],quiz:{q:'What is a useful first step before collecting data from a website?',options:['Send as many requests as possible','Check permission, terms, and available APIs','Assume robots.txt grants every legal right'],answer:1,why:'Understand permission and permitted alternatives before collection. Robots guidance is only one part of responsible access.'},source:'https://docs.scrapy.org/en/latest/intro/tutorial.html'
}
];
